; EXERCISE 1: SUBSETS
;
; Sets: theory/sets.kurt lists what you may use. Read it first.
;
; Prove each statement: replace the `todo` of its proof by your proof. Don't change the `show`
; lines. Check the file with
;
;     kurt 1-subsets.kurt
;
; until it says `Proof checked` (with `todo`s left it says `Proof almost checked`).
;
; How to prove `A ⊂ B`: take an arbitrary element of A with `let x ∈ A`, and prove `x ∈ B` in
; the block. The block gives `∀ x ∈ A (x ∈ B)`; write the next line `∀ x (x ∈ A ⇒ x ∈ B)`, and
; then `A ⊂ B` follows by the rule "subset". To use a fact `A ⊂ B`, write `∀ x (x ∈ A ⇒ x ∈ B)`
; as a line: then from `a ∈ A` follows `a ∈ B`.

load theory/sets
const A, B, C

; (a) Every set is a subset of itself.
show A ⊂ A
proof
    todo
qed

; (b) An intersection is a subset of each of its sets.
show A ∩ B ⊂ A
proof
    todo
qed

; (c) Each set is a subset of a union with it.
show A ⊂ A ∪ B
proof
    todo
qed

; (d) The empty set is a subset of every set.
;     Hint: in the block of `let x ∈ ∅` you have a contradiction. What follows from `false`?
show ∅ ⊂ A
proof
    todo
qed

; (e) A common subset of A and B is a subset of A ∩ B.
show C ⊂ A ∧ C ⊂ B ⇒ C ⊂ A ∩ B
proof
    todo
qed

; (f) The subset relation is transitive, for all sets X, Y, Z. The statement has a label: once it
;     is proven, the later parts can use it as a rule, "subset-trans".
;     Hint: `let X`, `let Y`, `let Z`, then `assume` the left side. (Name the elements in the
;     innermost block `x`: a name different from X, Y, Z.)
show ∀ X ∀ Y ∀ Z (X ⊂ Y ∧ Y ⊂ Z ⇒ X ⊂ Z)                    "subset-trans"
proof
    todo
qed

; (g) The power set is monotone: a larger set has more subsets (every element of Pow(A) is one of Pow(B)).
;     Hint: an element of Pow(A) is a subset of A ("power-set"). Then use (f) for it.
set X                      ; an element X of Pow(A) is a set (a fact gives no sort: say it)
show A ⊂ B ⇒ ∀ X ∈ Pow(A) (X ∈ Pow(B))
proof
    todo
qed
